encyclopedia :velkur do
  name "Velkur"
  summary "Velkur are amphibious ambush predators of Istrava's tidal caves, holding an air pocket against the roof while their jaws wait just beneath the surface."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :animal
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :danger, :subject_planetary_life
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :waterway] }
  biology "A broad amphibious body with four gripping limbs, paired side jaws and a ridged back that traps air against an overhang"
  principal_accommodation "Tidal caves or sheltered rocky shores with small aquatic prey and places to breathe"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "A mottled flat body hugs the ceiling above two long side jaws; trapped air forms a silver seam along its ridged back.", behavior: "The animal anchors above rising water, waits for prey below its head and drops bodily onto a large disturbance.", threat: "Side jaws close across a swimmer's shoulders while the gripping limbs hold the cave roof.", senses: "Surface ripples and nearby movement guide the first strike."
  prose <<~PROSE
    Velkur climb the low roofs of tidal caves before the water rises. Ridges along the back trap an air pocket against the stone, letting the animal wait above a nearly submerged passage. Its jaws hang below the surface. A fish passing through them can disappear without a splash; larger prey brings the whole body down from the roof.

    Boat crews around #{ref :kethra} look for a silver air seam that stays in one place while smaller bubbles pass. A pole touched to the roof may make the animal shift its grip, exposing its outline. Swimming beneath it with a lamp pointed downward leaves the most dangerous part of the passage unexamined. Outside caves, velkur rest beneath ledges and submerged root mats, returning to air before their trapped pocket is exhausted.

    The #{ref :ilvaren, "Ilvaren"} move juveniles away from crowded bathing pools using submerged bait cages. Adult relocation requires an empty receiving shore and people willing to keep others away until the animal settles. Residents of #{ref :oskara} sometimes object that a rescue has delivered a predator to their fishing ground. Velkur learn a repeated feeding place, but familiarity with a boat does not prevent a strike at the person entering its water.
  PROSE
  cue "A silver seam clings to the cave roof while the current carries every other bubble away."
  cue "Two narrow jaws turn beneath the water before the broad body above them becomes visible."
  affordance "Checking the roof and disturbing a suspected air pocket can expose a waiting velkur before someone enters the passage."
  pressure "Relocating a predator requires a receiving habitat whose human neighbors may refuse it."
  variation "Juveniles take small fish under shallow root mats and can be caught in bait cages."
  variation "Large cave adults span a narrow passage and remain submerged longer beneath a generous roof pocket."
end
